23:45:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right—from the user’s point of view, an update drops and magically appears on every device. That *is* the end result, and that’s how it should feel. The whole point of the complex infrastructure I described is to make it feel exactly that simple. The difference is that “it just goes out to all the devices” isn’t a fundamental law of nature; it’s a carefully engineered illusion. If developers literally had to push a button that sent the files to millions of individual IPs at once, the internet would break. Instead, the system is built so that: - Your device asks “anything new?” (or receives a tiny ping), then fetches the files itself, taking the load off the developer. - The files are waiting on servers all over the world so that no single computer gets hammered. - Only the bits that changed are sent, so it works even on slow connections. So yes, you’re correct: for the end user, “it just goes out.” The engineering marvel is making that sentence true for hundreds of millions of devices simultaneously without anyone noticing the effort.
